model response. After this response, maybe no more. So all work must be in this response. I can use code to do conditional logic and tests in one go.\n\nCould I read unknown portions within the code and make edits dynamically based on markers. Yes.\n\nLet me design code:\n\n1. Read environ.py, logger.py, stats_logger.py contents via read_file (already cached but fine).\n2. Inspect environ logs: print lines containing \"Logging\" and nearby; but can't see output before editing in same turn. Instead programmatically:\n   - Ensure helper function for max chars exists. Add module-level function `_int_min(v, minimum)`? Under descriptor. Better add factory method to EnvVar: `int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int)`. But tests may expect attribute. We can add static method `_int_or_none_min`.\n   - Locate the logging section within class envs. Need anchor. Could regex find a line like `    # -- Logging --` or `Logging` comments. Let's search within code and use pattern. If not, append at end of class envs? That's fragile.\n\nMaybe inspect repo via read-range in this same response impossible to see before write... but I can make code print and also execute edits conditional on known anchors found. If anchor missing, fallback robustly.\n\nWe can write a Python function to find the class envs block and insert before first \"class \" after it? Need inspect structure. Probably `class envs:` contains sections with comments like `# Logging`. I recall DeepInfer environ conventions from previous tasks: classes:\n\nclass envs:\n    # Logging\n    Logging_Level = EnvVar.str(default=\"INFO\")\n    Logging_File = EnvVar.str_or_none()\n...\n\nLet me maybe use search_code tool call in same action? Not useful for me to see now.\n\nCould use code that reads environ, prints the relevant lines, but also edits logger and stats (which I know), then prints environ snippet? Then still can't edit environ after seeing. But maybe code can handle insertion generically.\n\nLet's formulate robust insertion:\n- Find within class `envs` the line matching `^    #[^\\n]*Logging` (comment). Insert after that comment line: `    Logging_Max_Message_Chars = EnvVar.int_or_none(minimum=32)` or exact attribute name? Requirement: \"在 deepinfer/environ.py 的集中配置中加入同名属性\" — same name as env var `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. But existing config attributes likely names match env var names minus DEEPINFER_? Need know. The descriptor `__set_name__` uses attribute name as env var name. So if attribute must read DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS, class attribute must literally be `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`? Existing config names likely class attrs like `Logging_Level` reading `Logging_Level`? Hmm.\n\nWait descriptor name is attribute name; `os.getenv(self.name)`. So env var names equal attribute names. Maybe `class envs` attributes are uppercase like `LOG_LEVEL`? Need inspect. Could check likely DeepInfer. Similar tasks: deepinfer/environ.py maybe:\n\nclass envs:\n    # -- Ray --\n    RAY_DISABLE_STRICT_DEPS = EnvVar.bool(...)\n\nI can programmatically locate existing logging attribute names by scanning for `os.getenv`? Descriptor doesn't embed. Search lines in class for `EnvVar` and names containing \"LOG\".\n\nBut for tests, they may import `from deepinfer.environ import envs, rearm` and access `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`? Requirement says \"新增环境变量 DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS。...在 deepinfer/environ.py 的集中配置中加入同名属性\" — \"同名属性\" likely literally same name. Descriptor requires attr name equals env var. So add `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = ...` to envs class. But existing convention? If all attrs uppercase env names, fine. If not, this one should still be named exactly DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS for os.getenv to work. \"同名属性\" strongly indicates that.\n\nMaybe the tests access `envs.LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`? No, \"同名\" means same as env var. We'll add exact name. Could also add alias? Aliases would read another env var, bad. Could add property? Not needed.\n\nFactory validation: Set via `EnvVar(lambda v: ... )` returning None if v is None; int(v) raises ValueError; then if <32 raise ValueError. Error wrapped by descriptor to include name. Good. We can add static factory `int_or_none_min` or just inline lambda at class body:\n`DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: EnvVar[int | None] = EnvVar(lambda v: None if v is None else _int_min(v, 32))`\nNeed module helper `_int_min` defined. We can insert function near other helpers. Or lambda expression `(lambda v: None if v is None else (lambda x: x if x >= 32 else (_ for _ in ()).throw(ValueError(...)))(int(v)))` ugly.\n\nCan use existing `_int`? Need know its semantics. Probably `_int(v, default)` returns default if None. Not suitable. Could add static factory method to EnvVar using method code; class definition likely after helper. Define helper `_int_min` module-level by inserting right before `class EnvVar`. That is safe. Then class attr uses it.\n\nInsert config attribute: safest append to end of `class envs` body. Need locate class envs body. Could find `\\nclass envs` and next top-level line starting non-space (excluding decorators?). Append before next top-level definition. But there may be functions after class. We can use indentation scanning: from `class envs` line, find next line with non-empty and not starting whitespace and not comment? Insert at that position with `    DEEPINFER...`. But if there are top-level imports? Class body ends at next top-level `def` or `class` at col0. Fine.\n\nYet tests may expect config grouped but not necessary. Requirement \"集中配置中加入同名属性\", any place in class fine. But adding at end after other fields okay. However if class ends with methods or nested? likely only attrs. We'll insert before first top-level non-comment line after class.\n\nAlternatively insert right after `class envs:`? Attribute order not important but safe. We can insert immediately after class declaration, but preserve decorators? class envs maybe has docstring. Insert after line `class envs:` fine; if docstring next, inserting attr before docstring ruins docstring semantics (not functional). Better insert before a known logging comment if exists, else end of class.\n\nFor logger:\n- Add `max_message_chars` keyword-only. Signature: `def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None)`. Validate: if not None: `if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32: raise ValueError(...)`.\n- In format: Need truncate record.message after parameter substitution, before prefix alignment and before super().format? Requirement \"先完成 %s 参数替换，再计算消息长度\". `record.getMessage()` gives substituted message. But cannot mutate shared record. Approach: compute `message = record.getMessage()`, if budget and message too long and record.keep_full_message is not True: create a shallow copy of record? Or temporarily set record.msg to truncated, record.args = None, format, then restore? Requirement cannot rewrite shared record's msg,args,exc_info,stack_info; temporary mutation might be considered rewrite and not thread-safe. Better use `copy.copy(record)` and set msg to truncated on copy, args = (). Then `super().format(copy_record)`. But `record.message` used for multi-line alignment; copy's message? logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage(). After super().format(copy) it sets copy.message. We need use copy's message for alignment.\n\nNeed preserve exception info and stack_info (not truncated). copy.copy copies exc_info, stack_info references; super().format will format them fully appended. Good.\n\nImplementation:\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    effective = record\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and record.keep_full_message is not True and len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n        effective = copy.copy(record)\n        effective.msg = self._truncate_message(message)\n   